WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:02.860
Thanks to CuriosityStream for supporting this
episode!

00:00:02.860 --> 00:00:09.309
Go to CuriosityStream.com/microcosmos to start
streaming thousands of documentaries and for

00:00:09.309 --> 00:00:19.880
a special offer that helps support our show.

00:00:19.880 --> 00:00:24.450
If you’ve been following along with us lately,
you probably know that we’re pretty excited

00:00:24.450 --> 00:00:26.830
about our latest microscope upgrades.

00:00:26.830 --> 00:00:28.980
And can you blame us?

00:00:28.980 --> 00:00:32.689
It's like having a new toy that’s actually
a spaceship.

00:00:32.689 --> 00:00:36.570
It’s pretty hard to shut up about something
that is shiny and new, and also takes you

00:00:36.570 --> 00:00:39.070
into a whole new world.

00:00:39.070 --> 00:00:44.060
And that’s kind of what it feels like to
see our familiar friends at this magnification.

00:00:44.060 --> 00:00:48.330
Even though we’ve seen tardigrades many
times before, in so many different ways, we

00:00:48.330 --> 00:00:55.310
have not seen them like this.

00:00:55.310 --> 00:01:01.370
And bringing this level of magnification gives
us a new layer of the microcosmos to explore.

00:01:01.370 --> 00:01:07.500
It’s like we’ve descended into a new level
of a cave, getting further from our world—though

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:13.040
fortunately for the easily scared among us,
the cave is metaphorical and the risks are negligible.

00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:19.220
All we need for the journey are some objective
lenses and a tiny drop of... oil?

00:01:19.220 --> 00:01:21.740
Yeah, oil.

00:01:21.740 --> 00:01:26.940
But to explain what that oil does, we’re
going to have to get a little technical first.

00:01:26.940 --> 00:01:32.210
Because yes, we love our microbes, but we’re
only able to see them thanks to light, and

00:01:32.210 --> 00:01:35.560
light is complicated, but we need to give
it its due.

00:01:35.560 --> 00:01:38.650
So let’s start with the objective lens.

00:01:38.650 --> 00:01:44.780
The main job of the objective lens is to take
light leaving the sample and focus it into

00:01:44.780 --> 00:01:47.160
an image for the us to see.

00:01:47.160 --> 00:01:51.780
And there are two main numbers that describe
the objective lens.

00:01:51.780 --> 00:01:54.840
The first one’s pretty obvious: it’s the
magnification.

00:01:54.840 --> 00:01:59.140
But the magnification you see written on the
objective lens is not the same as the magnification

00:01:59.140 --> 00:02:01.509
we write up on the corner of the videos.

00:02:01.509 --> 00:02:06.400
That’s because the objective lens isn’t
the only source of magnification in our microscope.

00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:10.110
We’re also looking through an eyepiece that
has its own magnification.

00:02:10.110 --> 00:02:16.970
So the final magnification that we see is
the product of the objective lens’ and eyepiece’s magnifications.

00:02:16.970 --> 00:02:20.450
Now we don’t usually do math on this channel,
but this is one of the rare cases where the

00:02:20.450 --> 00:02:22.730
math and optics is straightforward.

00:02:22.730 --> 00:02:26.250
In our microscope, the eyepiece has a magnification
of 10x.

00:02:26.250 --> 00:02:32.500
So if we’re looking through a 63x objective,
the final magnification is 630x.

00:02:32.500 --> 00:02:35.730
And that is the number you’ll see in the
corner.

00:02:35.730 --> 00:02:40.260
But you should never trust an optics lesson
that seems simple.

00:02:40.260 --> 00:02:46.450
How could it be when the image we’re seeing
is the product of so many things at once,

00:02:46.450 --> 00:02:51.840
some tangible like a lens, others much less
so.

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:57.600
And what is less tangible than light, which
is, as we’ve seen before on this channel--a

00:02:57.600 --> 00:03:00.450
very strange thing.

00:03:00.450 --> 00:03:05.860
We cannot touch it, but we can manipulate
it, using what we know of the way it travels

00:03:05.860 --> 00:03:10.340
and bends and reflects to see our world differently.

00:03:10.340 --> 00:03:14.900
But light… has its limits.

00:03:14.900 --> 00:03:19.660
The thing about making things larger is that
at some point, it’s not enough to just zoom in.

00:03:19.660 --> 00:03:23.060
You need to be able to capture the detail
that’s there.

00:03:23.060 --> 00:03:30.709
And this leads to the second important number
you’ll find on an objective lens: the numerical aperture.

00:03:30.709 --> 00:03:35.980
Unlike magnification, “numerical aperture”
is probably not super self-explanatory.

00:03:35.980 --> 00:03:41.629
What the number describes is the ability of
the objective to take in light.

00:03:41.629 --> 00:03:47.930
As light travels from the sample and to the
objective, it radiates outwards like a cone.

00:03:47.930 --> 00:03:53.770
The higher your numerical aperture, the wider
the cone of light that's going into your objective.

00:03:53.770 --> 00:03:59.120
And that allows for more rays to enter the
objective from all sorts of different angles,

00:03:59.120 --> 00:04:04.170
helping to illuminate more details and give
greater resolution to your final image.

00:04:04.170 --> 00:04:11.160
But of course, that’s still not all that
goes into capturing the perfect microscopic image.

00:04:11.160 --> 00:04:17.259
Let’s take a look at this glaucoma spinning
around at 1000x magnification.

00:04:17.259 --> 00:04:21.030
It’s visible, and you can see some of the
details.

00:04:21.030 --> 00:04:22.300
It looks…fine.

00:04:22.300 --> 00:04:24.490
But let’s take a look at it again here.

00:04:24.490 --> 00:04:25.589
Same glaucoma.

00:04:25.589 --> 00:04:26.689
Same objective.

00:04:26.689 --> 00:04:28.349
Same level of magnification.

00:04:28.349 --> 00:04:33.400
What was “fine” before now just looks
dull in comparison to the image we were seeing

00:04:33.400 --> 00:04:38.589
before with the detailed striations and vivid
pockets of green on the glaucoma’s body.

00:04:38.589 --> 00:04:43.629
To get from one of these images to the other,
James—our master of microscopes—didn’t

00:04:43.629 --> 00:04:48.590
change any of the technical settings on his
microscope or pull some kind of video editing wizardry.

00:04:48.590 --> 00:04:54.770
He just simply added a drop of oil to the
coverslip encasing the glaucoma, and dipped

00:04:54.770 --> 00:04:57.620
the objective into the oil.

00:04:57.620 --> 00:05:04.589
Now this is because the cone of light going
from the specimen into the objective isn’t

00:05:04.589 --> 00:05:07.639
just going straight from the sample to the
lens.

00:05:07.639 --> 00:05:13.860
It’s passing through something—it’s
passing through air.

00:05:13.860 --> 00:05:18.619
Light travels at different speeds through
different materials and when it changes from

00:05:18.619 --> 00:05:21.009
material to material, it will bend.

00:05:21.009 --> 00:05:26.939
This is called refraction and materials like
oil slow light down more.

00:05:26.939 --> 00:05:32.169
And that matters to our microscope because
it gives us the wider cones of light we need

00:05:32.169 --> 00:05:34.889
to get a higher numerical aperture.

00:05:34.889 --> 00:05:38.639
Whether we’ve got air between the sample
and the lens or oil when we’re viewing our

00:05:38.639 --> 00:05:42.080
samples depends on the objective we’re looking
through.

00:05:42.080 --> 00:05:46.809
Our lower magnification objectives have lower
numerical apertures, and they’re meant to

00:05:46.809 --> 00:05:48.399
be used with air.

00:05:48.399 --> 00:05:53.080
Our higher magnification objectives have higher
numerical apertures, and they’re meant to

00:05:53.080 --> 00:05:55.169
be used with oil.

00:05:55.169 --> 00:06:00.800
Using air with oil objectives gives us poor
images, and using oil with our dry objective

00:06:00.800 --> 00:06:02.309
would damage the objective.

00:06:02.309 --> 00:06:03.699
So…don’t do that.

00:06:03.699 --> 00:06:08.259
The numerical aperture and its relationship
to resolution is important because it puts

00:06:08.259 --> 00:06:13.830
a limit on just how much magnification we
can actually do with a given objective.

00:06:13.830 --> 00:06:17.830
There’s a general estimate if you are looking
into microscopy yourself, that you’ll get

00:06:17.830 --> 00:06:22.669
a good magnification when you’re working
in the range that’s 500 to 1000 times the

00:06:22.669 --> 00:06:24.939
numerical aperture of the objective.

00:06:24.939 --> 00:06:31.639
So our 100x objective and 1.3 numerical aperture
can reach a maximum of 1300x magnification

00:06:31.639 --> 00:06:33.449
depending on your eyepiece.

00:06:33.449 --> 00:06:38.499
With our 10x eyepiece, we get 1000x magnification,
and it works great.

00:06:38.499 --> 00:06:42.599
But if we tried to get more from this set-up
by increasing the magnification of our eyepiece

00:06:42.599 --> 00:06:47.199
to 20x, we could not actually get 2000x magnification.

00:06:47.199 --> 00:06:51.990
Because of the constraints of our numerical
aperture, we would just be losing half of

00:06:51.990 --> 00:06:56.679
our image without actually gaining more detail
in what’s left to see.

00:06:56.679 --> 00:07:00.849
But that’s what happens any time we want
to take an image: we have to make a decision

00:07:00.849 --> 00:07:04.229
between what we can and cannot see.

00:07:04.229 --> 00:07:10.399
There are always choices that have to be made,
and details that have to be lost.

00:07:10.399 --> 00:07:16.020
We simply cannot see all of the world, in
all its entirety, all at once.

00:07:16.020 --> 00:07:22.679
But we can see more of it by tracking the
choices we make as we dive deeper, choices

00:07:22.679 --> 00:07:28.979
in techniques and materials that affect what
we can see and how we see it, and enrich the

00:07:28.979 --> 00:07:33.530
story further, even when those choices impose
constraints.

00:07:33.530 --> 00:07:40.159
At some point in history, we wanted to see
more, and lenses with their magnificent manipulation

00:07:40.159 --> 00:07:42.440
of light have helped us do that.

00:07:42.440 --> 00:07:48.899
We’ve used them to see light from distant
stars, and to peer into the most mundane surroundings

00:07:48.899 --> 00:07:50.139
on earth.

00:07:50.139 --> 00:07:55.239
And whatever image has come back to us has
brought the universe closer to view, even

00:07:55.239 --> 00:07:59.399
if it’s just in fractions.

00:07:59.399 --> 00:08:05.150
Thank you for coming on this journey with
us as we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.

